# Wrangler `.dev.vars` template — copy to `.dev.vars` for local development.
#
# `wrangler dev` (including the workerd-backed `pnpm dev` via
# `@cloudflare/vite-plugin`) auto-loads this file and exposes each entry as
# a Worker binding, mirroring `wrangler secret put` for production. Never
# commit the real `.dev.vars` — it is listed in `.gitignore`.
#
# Non-secret config (e.g. `APP_URL`) belongs in the matching
# `wrangler*.toml` `[vars]`, not here. Reserve this file for credentials
# and tokens that must not be checked in.
#
# === Which Worker owns which secret ======================================
#
# There are two Workers. The request Worker serves HTTP, does the
# CPU-bound work and hosts the queue consumers; the state Worker owns the
# Durable Object classes. A secret belongs to whichever one reads it.
#
#   SESSION_SECRET               — request Worker
#   MAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY        — request Worker (the mail consumer lives there)
#   DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET     — request Worker (canonical → full-length HMAC)
#   IDENTITY_MAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY — state Worker (never leaves the Durable Object)
#   PROVIDER_IDEMPOTENCY_KEY     — state Worker (never leaves the Durable Object)
#   IDENTITY_RESET_TOKEN_KEY     — state Worker (never leaves the Durable Object)
#   AI_CLIENT_TOKEN_SECRET       — request Worker (the AI API's tokens, codes and client ids)
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / _SECRET   — request Worker (the SSO callback runs there)
#   MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS            — not a secret: a [vars] entry; the deploy templates take it
#                                  from the MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS environment variable at render time
#   MAIL_DEV_SINK                — request Worker, **local only** (development mail sink)
#   SSO_DEV_STUB                 — request Worker, **local only** (development identity provider)
#
# **DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET and IDENTITY_MAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY are
# distributed to opposite Workers on purpose.** The request Worker can
# compute where a credential lives and cannot read any address back;
# the state Worker can read an address back and cannot compute where
# anything lives. Handing either key to both Workers collapses that
# split, and nothing in the code would notice.
#
# **Locally the split does not hold.** Both wrangler configs sit in this
# same directory and `.dev.vars` is resolved against the config's
# directory, so every entry of this file is handed to both Workers —
# measured with `wrangler dev -c wrangler.toml -c wrangler.state.toml`,
# where the state Worker receives `SESSION_SECRET` and
# `DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET` as well. The ownership above is what holds
# from staging onward, where each secret is installed with
# `wrangler secret put --config` against one Worker's config only.
#
# **This table is not the complete roster of secrets.** The three keys
# that never leave a Durable Object are all declared now; the
# **previous** keyring entry and the rotation key commitment are added by
# #67. `SESSION_SECRET` is listed above even though it predates this
# table, so that the table reads as "the secrets declared so far" rather
# than "all of them" — otherwise a later slice could take it for a
# complete list and drop its own entry.
#
# The two `*_DEV_*` entries are not secrets and are not installed
# anywhere: they are read from this file alone, and
# `wranglerConfig.test.ts` fails if a deployed config ever declares them.
#
# Each secret is installed against the config of the Worker that owns it:
#
#   wrangler secret put SESSION_SECRET               --config wrangler.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put MAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY        --config wrangler.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET     --config wrangler.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put IDENTITY_MAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY --config wrangler.state.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put PROVIDER_IDEMPOTENCY_KEY     --config wrangler.state.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put IDENTITY_RESET_TOKEN_KEY     --config wrangler.state.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put AI_CLIENT_TOKEN_SECRET       --config wrangler.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID             --config wrangler.staging.toml
#   wrangler secret put GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET         --config wrangler.staging.toml
#
# (substitute `wrangler.production.toml` / `wrangler.state.production.toml`
# for production.)
# =========================================================================

# HMAC key that signs session cookies. Left empty on purpose: a value
# published in this file would be a valid key for anyone who reads the
# repository, and copying it into a deployment hands out every account.
# Generate your own — the Worker refuses every request until you do:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# At least 32 characters. Owner: request Worker.
SESSION_SECRET=""

# Credential of the transactional mail provider. Owner: **request
# Worker** — the mail consumer runs in its `queue()` handler, so the
# provider is called from there and not from a Durable Object.
#
# Locally `MAIL_DEV_SINK` below selects a sink that calls no provider, so
# this may stay empty; from staging onward it is the Resend API key, and
# the sender address is `MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS` in the stage's `[vars]`.
MAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY=""

# Development mail sink. `"console"` makes the mail consumer print
# `[dev-mail] to=<address> url=<reset link>` on the request Worker's log
# instead of calling a provider — the declared exception to the payload
# hygiene rule (`spec/async/index.md`), for the browser check of the
# password reset only. Any other value is a configuration error; leave it
# unset to use the provider. **Local only**: no deployed config declares it.
MAIL_DEV_SINK="console"

# Development identity provider. `"true"` replaces every SSO provider with
# the in-app stub at `/__dev/sso/<provider>/authorize`, which asks for a
# subject and an email instead of talking to Google. **Local only**: no
# deployed config declares it, and the stub route answers 404 without it.
SSO_DEV_STUB="true"

# Google OAuth client for the real `google` provider (unused while
# `SSO_DEV_STUB` is `"true"`). Owner: **request Worker** — the callback
# handler exchanges the code there. Register
# `${APP_URL}/auth/sso/google/callback` as the redirect URI.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# HMAC key that maps a canonical credential to its full-length value, from
# which the Identity Directory bucket is chosen. Owner: **request
# Worker** — bucket selection happens in the stub-selection adapter,
# before any Durable Object is entered, which is why this key is
# deliberately *not* one of the three that stay inside a DO.
#
# It is the key material of the **active** keyring entry. The generation
# number and the bucket count are not variables: they are properties of
# the generation itself, fixed in
# `adapters/cloudflare/crypto/keyring.ts`, because changing the bucket
# count re-buckets every credential already stored and is therefore a new
# generation rather than a new setting. The `previous` entry and the
# rotation commitment arrive with #67.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# At least 32 characters.
DIRECTORY_ROUTING_SECRET=""

# AES-256-GCM key protecting the canonical credential value
# (`credential_mappings.encrypted_canonical`). Owner: **state Worker** —
# it never leaves the Identity Directory Durable Object, which is what
# keeps a stored address unreadable to the request Worker and to anything
# that only reaches the routing key.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# At least 32 characters. Only one generation exists; re-encryption under
# a second one arrives with #67.
IDENTITY_MAIL_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# HMAC key from which each Durable Object derives the
# `providerIdempotencyKey` it hands back in the send-materials response.
# Owner: **state Worker**. It never leaves the Durable Object: the
# consumer receives the finished key and can neither derive nor verify it.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
PROVIDER_IDEMPOTENCY_KEY=""

# HMAC key from which each Identity Directory bucket derives the secret
# half of a password-reset token from its `token_id`, so that the raw
# token is never stored and can be re-derived for the send-materials RPC.
# Owner: **state Worker**. It never leaves the Durable Object.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# At least 32 characters.
IDENTITY_RESET_TOKEN_KEY=""

# HMAC secret of the AI API (OAuth 2.1 + MCP). Every signed value the
# request Worker hands an AI client — access and refresh tokens, the
# authorization code, the stateless `client_id`, the authorization
# request the consent screen carries — is signed under a key derived from
# this one secret with a per-use label, so no two uses share a key.
# Owner: **request Worker** — the OAuth endpoints and the AI API run
# there. Rotating it invalidates every issued token and client id at once.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# At least 32 characters. The request path refuses every request until
# it is set.
AI_CLIENT_TOKEN_SECRET=""
